# Example torrc for onioncrawler.
#
#   tor -f /path/to/torrc
#
# Two things:
#  1) A SOCKS port with stream isolation, so onioncrawler's per-host SOCKS
#     username produces a separate Tor circuit per .onion host (circuit reuse
#     per host, isolation across hosts).
#  2) A v3 hidden service that publishes the local no-JS search UI as an .onion,
#     so the index is itself reachable over Tor without exposing any clearnet
#     port.

# --- crawling: isolating SOCKS proxy ---
SocksPort 127.0.0.1:9050 IsolateSOCKSAuth KeepAliveIsolateSOCKSAuth

# Optional: a second, dedicated isolating SOCKS port.
# SocksPort 127.0.0.1:9052 IsolateSOCKSAuth

# --- publishing the search UI as a hidden service ---
# Run:  onioncrawler search --db /var/lib/onioncrawler/crawl.db \
#           --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8802
# Tor will map <your-address>.onion:80 -> 127.0.0.1:8802.
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/onioncrawler_search/
HiddenServiceVersion 3
HiddenServicePort 80 127.0.0.1:8802

# After first start, the onion address is in:
#   /var/lib/tor/onioncrawler_search/hostname

# Hardening: do not run as an exit or relay.
ExitPolicy reject *:*
